From human_reviews/Zonhl0c9I0.md on reward learning:
> "The Experimental design is very narrow, and limited. (see questions for more details). E.g, I would expect to see results on downstream/standard benchmarks. However currently only preference accuracy is reported."

### Weakness 2: Significant gap between theoretical analysis and empirical evaluation

The theoretical regret bounds in Theorems 5.1 and 5.2 require restrictive assumptions (linear reward function in trajectory features, tabular or discrete state spaces, known dynamics model) that do not hold in the experimental environments, which feature high-dimensional continuous state spaces and non-linear reward functions. The paper acknowledges this gap (line 514: "Our settings largely involve high-dimensional state spaces where the ground-truth reward function is not linear") but the disconnect limits the practical utility of the theoretical contributions.

**Retrieved review quote supporting this weakness:**
From human_reviews/bGkPZtisSm.md on DPO generalization:
> "The theoretical analysis decomposes the model output into the product of W g(x), whereas we only consider the dynamic of W. It is reduced to a linear analysis... Therefore, the two main bounds look fine, but these too points make the theoretical analysis not very strong."

From human_reviews/v4MTnPiYXY.md on Q-learning for LLMs:
> "Reuse of the pretrained weights is emphasized as the main motivation, but some of the tasks actually train the model from scratch and others are not in natural language, which is a bit conflicting. There is still an improvement when trained from scratch, which is good, but there is a lack of explanation why it works better."

From human_reviews/hNjCVVm0EQ.md on time-varying systems:
> "The framework assumes that the Mamba-generated matrices adequately capture the dynamics of the underlying system. This assumption may not hold for highly nonlinear systems where matrix representations could become unstable or inaccurate... The paper could use more rigorous theoretical analysis or proofs to back the claims of stability and effectiveness."

### Weakness 3: Insufficient analysis of key assumptions and their impact

The paper makes several critical assumptions that may not hold broadly: (1) the proxy reward function is typically "optimistic" (line 265-271), (2) the Bradley-Terry preference model accurately captures human preferences, and (3) preferences are elicited over full trajectories rather than shorter segments. While the paper shows PBRR can still work when the optimism assumption is violated (Glucose Monitoring environment), there is insufficient analysis of when and why these assumptions may fail, and how robust the method is to such violations.

