## Strengths

1. **Well-motivated approach**: The paper addresses an important problem in probabilistic time series forecasting by relaxing the Gaussian assumption commonly made in diffusion-based methods, while providing flexibility in model selection.

2. **Theoretical grounding**: The paper provides theoretical justification for the validity of the resampling procedure (Theorem 1) and explains why the two-stage training procedure can learn the conditional mean and volatility functions.

3. **Comprehensive experimental validation**: The paper includes experiments on six univariate and three multivariate real-world datasets with confidence intervals based on five independent repetitions.

## Weaknesses

### 1. **Missing Comparisons with Recent Probabilistic Forecasting Methods**

The experimental evaluation only compares models with and without DualRes (ablation study format), but lacks comparison with other recent probabilistic forecasting methods beyond the baseline models.

**Retrieved review quote (from human_reviews/WFlLqUmb9v.md):**
> "The literature review and comparison with existing methods are notably incomplete. While some papers, such as the one available at https://arxiv.org/html/2407.21275v1, are cited, the authors do not compare their model against these works. Furthermore, other relevant papers (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2403.11047v1 and https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0167404824002669) that utilize temporal correlations between STFT windows have been overlooked, undermining the claimed novelty of the presented method."

**Retrieved review quote (from human_reviews/xTrAA3UKPa.md):**
> "In the experiments, the proposed algorithm is only compared with the traditional genetic algorithm. It would be beneficial to evaluate other hyperparameter search techniques, such as the classic sliding window search and k-fold validation technique."

The current paper would benefit from comparing DualRes against other recent probabilistic forecasting methods such as state-space models or other diffusion-based approaches mentioned in the related work to better demonstrate its advantages.

### 2. **Lack of Ablation Studies on Individual Components**

While the paper demonstrates that adding DualRes improves performance, it does not isolate the contribution of its two main components: (1) conditional volatility modeling and (2) resampling of normalized residuals.

**Retrieved review quote (from human_reviews/LqB8cRuBua.md):**
> "This paper lacks ablation experiments. Additional experiments are needed to verify the role of each component of Sigformer."

**Retrieved review quote (from human_reviews/Tepaft7632.md):**
> "There is no ablation study in the paper to demonstrate the independent function of learnable cluster center and one-directed adaptive loss."

An ablation study showing performance with: (a) only volatility modeling without resampling, (b) only resampling with homoskedastic assumptions, and (c) both components would strengthen the paper's claims about which aspect contributes most to performance improvements.

### 3. **No Statistical Significance Testing**

Although the paper provides 95% confidence intervals, it does not include statistical significance tests to determine whether the observed improvements are statistically significant or could be due to random variation.

**Retrieved review quote (from human_reviews/OovfCS4FYT.md):**
> "For ImageNet-C I don't understand why the authors only evaluate their AlexNet model... At minimum we would like to see the robustness results transfer across these two architectures but it is unclear from the text whether this is true. For the ImageNet-C robustness results, while I greatly appreciate the use of statistical significance, I would like to know what these results look like when averaging across the corruption severity levels..."

**Retrieved review quote (from human_reviews/cojJ2s1e35.md):**
> "No runtime analysis. No significance tests. No error bars... Unfortunately, the empirical evaluation does not meet scientific standards and, therefore, I do not deem the paper ready for publication yet."

**Retrieved review quote (from human_reviews/Tepaft7632.md):**
> "Table 1 shows consistent improvements for MADCluster over baselines across datasets, but the gains appear marginal (if we compare the best performer with the second best one, for example) and might lack statistical significance. Including standard deviations for these results would help validate the claims of performance improvement."

### 4. **Missing Runtime and Computational Complexity Analysis**

Section 6 mentions "One main limitation of our work lies in the computational complexity of the algorithm," but the paper provides no empirical runtime analysis or detailed computational complexity comparison with baseline methods.

**Retrieved review quote (from human_reviews/cojJ2s1e35.md):**
> "No runtime analysis... No scaling laws... Unfortunately, the empirical evaluation does not meet scientific standards."

**Retrieved review quote (from human_reviews/Tepaft7632.md):**
> "The paper lacks a detailed analysis of time and computational costs, particularly a comparison between the original base model and the model combined with MADCluster."

**Retrieved review quote (from human_reviews/WFlLqUmb9v.md):**
> "There is a short discussion on model complexity, but no experiment was performed to demonstrate the efficiency."

Given that DualRes requires training two separate models (for mean and volatility) and performing resampling B times during inference, quantitative runtime comparisons would help practitioners understand the practical trade-offs.

### 5. **Limited Novelty - Combination of Existing Techniques**

The core contribution combines well-known techniques: two-stage modeling (ARMA-GARCH style) and bootstrap resampling. The innovation appears primarily in the combination rather than in fundamentally new methodology.

**Retrieved review quote (from human_reviews/LqB8cRuBua.md):**
> "The contributions of this paper are limited. Diffusion and transformers have been proposed for a long time. For Sigformer, transformer and convolution are just simple combinations. The method proposed in this paper seems to be a patchwork of existing techniques."

**Retrieved review quote (from human_reviews/qZz7PKt4bE.md):**
> "The novelty is lacking. This research takes a pretrained model and applies LoRA finetuning to it with a new method of hyperparameter tuning. Most of these concepts are not new for time-series analysis."

**Retrieved review quote (from human_reviews/6Mdvq0bPyG.md):**
> "Not enough novelty. The main contribution appears to be the proposed training pipeline, but this pipeline does not introduce substantial advancements beyond existing techniques. While the combination of block-wise and end-to-end training is interesting, it builds on straightforward adaptations of known methods rather than providing an innovative or fundamentally new approach."

### 6. **Limited Evaluation on State-of-the-Art Baselines**

Several baselines (DLinear, PatchTST, TimeMixer) were originally designed for mean forecasting and adapted to probabilistic forecasting via t-distribution fitting. Comparison with methods specifically designed for probabilistic forecasting would be more informative.

**Retrieved review quote (from human_reviews/Tepaft7632.md):**
> "In the experimental part, MADCluster is mainly compared with methods such as D-RNN and USAD, but these baseline models do not cover the latest self-supervised or GAN-based anomaly detection methods, which may outperform the traditional methods on multimodal time series data. To ensure the comprehensiveness of the experimental results, it is recommended to introduce the latest baseline models to support the effectiveness of MADCluster."

**Retrieved review quote (from human_reviews/thV5KRQFgQ.md):**
> "The dynamic GNN models used in the evaluation are relatively outdated, potentially limiting the generalizability of the results... Could you share more evaluation results of the approach on recent models to provide a broader validation of its effectiveness?"
