## Review of VSSFlow: Unifying Video-Conditioned Sound and Speech Generation via Joint Learning

### Strengths

**1. Novel unified framework with effective conditioning mechanism**
The paper presents a well-motivated approach to unifying video-to-sound and visual text-to-speech generation within a single flow-matching framework, with a thoughtful conditioning aggregation mechanism that leverages different attention mechanisms for different modalities.

**2. Strong empirical results on both tasks**
VSSFlow achieves competitive or state-of-the-art performance on both V2S (VGGSound) and VisualTTS (Chem, GRID) benchmarks, demonstrating the effectiveness of the unified approach.

**3. Valuable insights on joint learning**
The paper provides interesting findings about mutual enhancement between sound and speech generation tasks, challenging prior beliefs about multi-task learning degradation.

### Weaknesses

**1. Unfair comparison due to different training data across baselines**

The comparison with baselines appears problematic because different methods use different training datasets. VSSFlow is trained only on V2S, VisualTTS, and TTS datasets without additional data, while MMAudio uses extra high-quality text-to-sound data (AudioCaps & WavCaps), and several baselines use AudioSet. This makes it difficult to assess whether performance differences stem from the method itself or from data advantages.

> **Quote from human review (gRmWtOnTLK.md):** "While the experimental sections is done in a good way as mentioned in the Strengths part, the fact that the authors trained RFWave on their proposed large-scale dataset (combining Common Voice 7.0, DNS Challenge 4, MTG-Jamendo, FSD50K and AudioSet) and that Vocos, BigVGAN, EnCodec, and MBD are evaluated using the public pre-trained models is somehow problematic: it could be that Vocos, BigVGAN, EnCodec, and MBD are under-trained with respect to the authors dataset. I would like the author to comment on this and at least show that the training sources are comparable."

**2. Limited evaluation scope and narrow benchmark coverage**

The evaluation is restricted to a small number of benchmarks: only VGGSound for V2S and only Chem/GRID for VisualTTS. There is no evaluation on other V2S datasets or more diverse real-world scenarios that would better demonstrate the model's generalization capabilities.

> **Quote from human review (YFKH1vO0W2.md):** "The paper touches on the problem of disentangled representation for sequential data in general. However, the presented experiments are limited - they only make use of simple (single person) video data. This does not give a fair picture of the methods usefulness and applicability because (i) it targets only a single modality; and (ii) because even within this modality the model targets very structured datasets (e.g. single view, single object)."

> **Quote from human review (m29SV0n6DO.md):** "The major experiments in the paper are in-domain. The model was trained on ImageNet, Kinetics and Ego4D and later tested on those three datasets. The DAVIS experiment is out-of-domain and novel and appreciated... It would have made the paper much stronger to have evaluated those tasks as a sign of the strength of joint vision encoding and generation."

**3. Joint sound-speech generation only demonstrated on synthetic data**

The paper's key claim about unified sound-speech joint generation (Section 4.5) is only validated using synthetic data created by "randomized concatenation or overlay" of separate sound and speech samples. No evaluation is provided on real-world videos containing natural sound-speech mixtures, which limits confidence in the practical applicability of this capability.

> **Quote from human review (thV5KRQFgQ.md - about synthetic vs real-world data):** Although this review is about a different domain, it raises similar concerns: "Experiments are mostly shown on short sequence face/motion datasets... Under the framework that the author proposed, ideally longer sequences should facilitates the disentanglement."

**4. Insufficient ablation studies on key design choices**

While the paper ablates conditioning mechanisms (Section 4.2), several important design choices remain unablated: (1) the choice of CLIP for video features vs. other visual encoders, (2) phoneme embedding dimensions and architectures, (3) the number and configuration of DiT layers, and (4) the impact of RoPE positioning. These missing ablations make it harder to understand which components are critical.

> **Quote from human review (DJSZGGZYVi.md):** "Some design choices appear arbitrary and lack supporting evidence. For example, in Section 4.1, the authors discuss the preference for Adaptive Double Normalization over AdaGN, but there is no experimental evidence supporting this choice. It would be more insightful to add a Figure similar to Figure 5 show experimental comparison..."

> **Quote from human review (gRmWtOnTLK.md):** "I find it difficult to understand the rationale behind... This could also be considered a potential drawback... The paper claims improved performance... but it is not clearly justified why it is effective."

**5. Limited analysis of why joint learning works**

The paper claims that joint learning of sound and speech leads to mutual enhancement (Section 4.3), attributing this to "learned general audio prior" and improved CFG stability. However, the mechanistic understanding of why this occurs is superficial. Deeper analysis of what is being shared, how the tasks interact during training, and what specific representations enable this synergy would strengthen the contribution.

> **Quote from human review (m29SV0n6DO.md):** "I think the analysis is missing some important pieces. While there are a lot of experiments, the takeaway and why is not really answered. For example, why is dVAE and patch-dVAE worse than VQGAN? Is the difference entirely due to label leak through perceptual loss?... In general, my criticism is that for the results section, a lot of results are presented without much analysis or experiments to explain why the results are there, which really limits the insight of the paper."

**6. VAE-vocoder quality loss acknowledged but not mitigated**

The paper acknowledges that the VAE-vocoder "incurs detail loss, reducing UTMOS versus ground truth" (Section 4.4), but does not explore alternatives, quantify the extent of this limitation, or propose solutions. This is a significant practical limitation that affects the real-world usability of the approach.

> **Quote from human review (FyMjfDQ9RO.md):** "Compared to prior studies [2,3], this paper's speech resynthesis (token-to-speech) method is significantly more complicated. It involves a CFM model to generate low-level articulatory features for the Articulatory Encodec. The complicated resynthesis method might introduce more uncertainties to the evaluation results. Also, the resynthesis intelligibility and quality are not significantly better than HuBERT with K-means clustering (Table 2)."

**7. Generalization to out-of-domain scenarios underexplored**

The joint generation capability is only qualitatively demonstrated on a small number of Veo3 videos. There is no systematic evaluation of how the model performs on diverse out-of-domain video types, different acoustic environments, or challenging scenarios like overlapping speech and sound.

> **Quote from human review (gRmWtOnTLK.md):** "Although Table 3 demonstrates better performance of RFWave on out-of-domain data, I still have doubts that such experimental setting is practical: in a real-life scenario one would rather re-train a waveform generation model on the target domain (music in this case) than use a model trained on an out-of-domain data (speech). I would consider testing performance... to get better understanding of advantages... brought by its better performance on out-of-domain data."

---

### Summary

This review is grounded in weaknesses observed in similar multimodal generation, audio synthesis, and unified model papers from the human review dataset. The key concerns revolve around comparison fairness, evaluation comprehensiveness, reliance on synthetic data for key claims, and insufficient mechanistic analysis of why the unified approach works.
